WEBVTT

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.280
When it comes to dealing with the environment, what is the

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.000
biggest risk that you can be exposed to if you're not very

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.600
well prepared for it? Because being prepared for

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.400
something like that is only experience, and experience comes

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.200
from trying and getting to know about things.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.880
So there's no ideal way to go out and saying that, yes, this

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.160
is how I will do it unless you've actually done it a few

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.400
times. And then they're like, no, OK, I

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.160
need to fine tune it a bit. So I totally agree with you and

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.720
many people would say, I would say this because it's my

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.240
passion, but I would say don't underestimate these

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.320
environmental factors. I think we have a tendency to do

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.840
that. If we think about the

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.800
environmental factors that can affect us, they're really oxygen

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.560
level, cold G force and sun exposure.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.520
I would say like those are the main ones.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:06.360
The oxygen thing is interesting because I've flown a lot in

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.200
India. It's my favorite place to fly.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.400
I was fortunate to fly in Pakistan a few years ago and

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.800
that was wonderful as well. And to go up nice and high.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.480
Undoubtedly low levels of oxygen affect us.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.000
They affect our ability to think.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.520
They affect in some cases, if it gets low enough, our ability to

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.320
see effectively in terms of our peripheral vision.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.080
All of those things narrow. They affect our ability to

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.800
control the glider. And they also have a bit of a

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.360
hangover effect. So if you get hypoxic and then

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.400
you go down to a lower altitude, you feel better, but you're

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.480
still not back to 100% for probably up to about 90 minutes.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.080
For those wondering what's hypoxic?

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.000
So hypoxia is low levels of oxygen in the blood.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.160
Technically the term for that is hypoxemia, but I would think

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.200
about it as we're in an environment when we fly high

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.160
where the pressure is low. That means that the oxygen

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.720
molecules, there's still 20% oxygen, same as there is at sea

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.640
level, but they're all further apart.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.800
So that means to get the same amount in, you have to breathe

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.960
faster, which is why actually flying at house 2 is so

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.800
dehydrating, because what's happening is you're breathing in

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.520
and out lots and you're shedding water each time you do so that

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.320
that's one of the reasons why it's dehydrating.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.800
The other reason is because the oxygen levels are lower.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.120
Your kidneys don't work as well, so you don't conserve water in

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.000
the same way, which is why you desperately need to pee when you

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.400
fly at high altitude or in cold environments.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.600
So oxygen's definitely a thing. It's hard to know what height it

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.120
starts to matter at because that depends quite a lot on different

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.880
people and it also depends on and by different people.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.640
Climatisation, sorry, genetics and fitness, it also depends on

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.960
a climatisation. We'll talk about that in a SEC.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.320
The there's a very easy way to fix it, which is to carry

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.920
supplementary oxygen, But there's also a lot of associated

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.960
faff that comes with carrying supplementary oxygen.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.000
But I can only say from personal experience that when I flew with

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.880
it in Pakistan, when I switched it on, suddenly my mood

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.280
improved. I felt warmer.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.960
I could think more clearly. I almost felt like I could.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.520
I don't know if I could physically see better.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.600
I just felt less blinkered. But I felt a striking increase

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.200
in bandwidth. So it's changed my perception a

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.240
little bit in terms of I knew academically that hypoxia was an

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.520
issue. It's first, it takes a while to

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.960
really feel it, and when you feel it, you're like OK.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.160
Or when you feel it going away or improving with oxygen, it

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.080
strikes you how much it might be affecting you.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.040
What are the first symptoms that come to you that you can

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.200
understand? Because as we spoke about in the

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.040
beginning of the show, that it's such a cognitively heavy load

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.400
and there's so much to look and observe and be constantly

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.480
thinking that it's so easy to be drifted away.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.960
So how do you identify that this might be coming in the next

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.200
10-15 minutes? This is why your point about

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.399
experience is such a good one, because everybody has their own.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:21.560
It's called a hypoxic signature. And so if you're a fighter

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.800
pilot, you get put in a chamber, you get made hypoxic and then

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.760
you get a load of puzzles and other things to do.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.400
And there's some brilliant videos of all these like

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.200
incredibly macho U.S. Marine pilots, just like mashing

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.800
like kids toys together. It's fun to watch.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.560
But the purpose of that training for them is both to realise that

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.120
they don't know they're impaired, but also to try and

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.760
work out exactly what you said, like how did they feel in the

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.440
the time leading up to that impairment?

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.680
Because it varies. And so finding our own hypoxic

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.000
signature as paraglider pilots is a little bit difficult

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.880
because we don't have access to these chambers.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:04.880
But I think one of the way to do it is to really try and be

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.320
observant of yourself when you go into these environments.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.160
So if say I'm in beer, I'm going over the back, I'm flying up

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:18.360
above 4000 metres asking myself what am I feeling, what can I

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.560
see, what can I think? And actually, because I was

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.680
quite interested in this, I put a little note on my cockpit to

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.800
remind me to think about what I'm thinking because you can get

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.080
so in the zone. But typically for people, they

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.400
would say things like mood changes.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.480
So you either feel pretty euphoric or you feel a bit sad

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.200
or a bit constrained or a bit afraid.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.960
Not having as big a bandwidth, some changes in motor skills, so

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.680
you're not thermalling as nicely as you would normally.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.160
You're bouncing in and out. Then as you get more hypoxic,

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.920
the way you communicate with others might be a thing.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.000
So sometimes people become less commutative, sometimes they

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.560
become quite garrulous, sometimes they start talking a

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.160
bit of nonsense. Certainly if that's happening,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.520
it's too late. But that's also the advantage of

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.080
flying with buddies because they can point out to you that this

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.800
thing is happening. Obviously, the most kind of

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.920
telling sign that it might be happening is just the fact that

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.520
you are high. So taking a note of your

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.440
altitude and think that OK, that I'm now entering A vulnerable

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:21.760
place. And I would say probably about

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.280
for most people because this varies a lot between

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.360
individuals. Once you start flying above 3000

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.520
meters, certainly 3 1/2, you are going to be subject to this all

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.440
right. Let's say how many minutes above

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.720
3000? Because a lot of times we are, I

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.040
wouldn't say exactly dolphining. You're getting high, lower,

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.520
high, lower, like depending on the glides.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.880
So when do you start hitting the point of no return that OK, now

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.480
I'm pushing my limits and then hypoxia is a very real thing now

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.360
because I've been doing this for like past two hours.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.240
So again, there's no evidence specific to paragliders, but

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.400
that I guess the aviation like general aviation would be above

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:07.280
10,000 feet for 30 minutes or above 13,000 feet for any period

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.440
of time. So I don't know what 10,000 feet

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.600
is that about 2802 thousand 600 metres, something like that.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.440
But that's the general aviation guidance.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.480
But it's so individual, like if you're, again, if you're tired,

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.080
hungover, dehydrated, your tolerance is going to be worse

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.600
if you're acclimatised. And a climate like whether

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.040
paragliding is acclimatising or not isn't really known.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.680
But the thing that is most acclimatising is the altitude at

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.000
which you sleep. So if you sleep at high

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.880
altitude, you know, a little bit higher than normal or closer to

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.560
the altitude at which you fly, then that is going to help you

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.560
tolerate the hypoxia. The really interesting 1 though,

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.240
for me though is cold. Because when we think about

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.680
hypoxia, we're thinking about supply what's available in the

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.560
atmosphere, but also demand how much your body is using.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.800
And it's that balance or that lack of balance that creates the

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.360
problems. And if you start shivering, your

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:18.840
oxygen consumption goes up by about five times, and oxygen is

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:26.000
being used to keep you warm. And if you can make sure that

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.600
you're stay nice and warm and you certainly don't shiver, then

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.799
I think that's one of the most helpful things you can do to

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:37.600
give yourself more margin when you're flying.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:38.960
You're in a hypoxic environment.
